form human actors into  gringotts goblins  the makeup team started their work at  4am  they created molds of each actor's face  and created a combination of rubber  masks  and individual prosthetic parts to  attach one by one  the actors also had to have their hands  covered with extensive makeup and  prosthetic pieces  long fake fingernails and dentures to  finish off the look  each goblin took four hours to get ready  and they even wore  contact lenses that obscured their  vision so much that they needed  chaperones to help get them around the  set on this one we're doing 45  full prosthetic makeups british actor  warwick davis  not only played professor flitwick the  first goblin bank teller but  also grip hook in the harry potter  series very unusual was it  to make sure he was unrecognizable from  one character to the next  it took hours and hours in the makeup  chair first  they created a cast of his face which  involved sitting under  layers of silicone and plaster for hours  and only his nose holes free to breathe  once the prosthetic pieces for grip hook  were applied warwick said he had to  re-learn how to use his face  underneath all those extra layers and  totally adjust his voice to speak  through the false teeth  hello ready for the office in order to  make the actor who play dumbledore's  brother aberforth look as much like  michael gambin as possible  they used a lot of makeup and extensive  wigs  the makeup crew added silicone to his  forehead and face  to push back his hairline applied  prosthetic bags under his eyes  completely changed his eyebrows and of  course added his long hair and beard to  finish off the look  it was quite interesting because in a  way you're working on a  blank canvas even though you might think  it was cgi  the scene in which harry accidentally  blows up his aunt marge  was all done in camera it took 20 people  to build and apply the inflatable suit  to pam ferris  the actress who played ant marge and  included pumps  de-